            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"A symbol of the quintessential wicked and oppressive pagan city, representing both the extreme depths of worldly depravity and the universal scope of God's mercy when its inhabitants repent, as well as the certainty of divine judgment upon arrogant empires.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Jonah 1:2\",\"Jonah 3:1-10\",\"Nahum 3:1-7\",\"Matthew 12:41\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"An archetype of the repentant sinner and a foreshadowing of Gentile inclusion in God's salvation.\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 12:41\",\"Luke 11:32\",\"Jonah 3:5-10\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A symbol of unrestrained imperial cruelty, worldly pride, and the inevitable destruction of the enemies of God's people.\",\"basis\":[\"Nahum 1:1-3\",\"Nahum 3:1-19\",\"Zephaniah 2:13-15\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"Nineveh functions as a dual symbol in biblical theology. In the tradition of Jonah and the Synoptic Gospels, it represents the boundless nature of God's grace toward repentant Gentiles. In the prophetic tradition of Nahum and Zephaniah, it stands as the ultimate tyrannical and bloodthirsty empire destined for divine wrath.\"}",
